tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{GB vs.\ RF deployment trade-off.}
GB leads RF by 5.5\,pp on all-windows cross-agent transfer (0.967 vs.\ 0.912), but RF is more robust on conf${\geq}$2 (0.996 vs.\ 0.992), under 2-LF label degradation ($-$1.8\,pp vs.\ $-$12\,pp), and in multi-seed variance (${\pm}0.000$ vs.\ ${\pm}0.002$).
Practitioners should prefer GB for broad deployment and RF for high-confidence or label-degraded settings.

\paragraph{Structural features vs.\ cycle detection.}
The cycle detector's in-domain gold parity with structural GB (${\sim}$0.84; \S\ref{sec:gold_set}) might suggest that simple repetition detection suffices.
However, the 43.7\,pp cross-agent gap (0.530 vs.\ 0.967) reveals that overt n-gram repetition is agent-specific: OpenHands stagnation manifests through diverse, non-repeating command sequences (Appendix~\ref{sec:app_cycle_detector}).
The structural features that generalize encode higher-order patterns---edit ratios, output entropy, file operation diversity---that persist across agent architectures.

\paragraph{Early termination.}
Stagnation detection enables compute savings via early termination.
A Pareto analysis (Appendix Figure~\ref{fig:pareto}) shows that structural GB achieves 15--30\% compute reduction with $<$2\% task resolution loss, while the probe requires target-agent fine-tuning to reach comparable savings.

